hen chemical photography gave way to  digital before entire systems of  knowledge would be lost funding in this  area was inclusive of all of the top  categories as you've heard the u.s.  currently has four graduate art  conservation programs that produce the  majority of practitioners in the  melon is one of many funders that  support the programs though probably the  largest and most consistent over time  historically the foundation has taken an  approach of relative equity toward these  programs and distribution of resources  to ensure a stable ecosystem that is in  balance with market supply and demand  and to produce a reasonable level of  consistency and skillset and proficiency  among graduates and Debbie spoke to this  very eloquently earlier the cost of an  advanced degree in conservation is high  three to four years of graduate study  are required to build the solid  foundation of knowledge and skills and  full time schedules of lectures seminars  research and laboratory work prevents  students from supplementing their  resources through outside employment  most students except unpaid thank you to  Yale for having paid pre-program  professional internships before they  even enroll in a graduate program and  must pay course fees for chemistry art  history and studio art to supplement  their preparation to acquire necessary  for requisites for admission moreover  once enrolled students frequently take  unpaid or low paid summer internships in  order to gain essential training  graduates enter an extremely competitive  and oftentimes a low-paying job market  where candidates commonly spend a number  of years in fellowships and placements  before securing an entry-level position  in a museum library or in private  practice the funding highlighted here  has gone largely to support student  stipends curriculum development faculty  positions and some specialized  programming Mellon grants do not support  tuition this must either be paid by the  student or the schools are must find  other forms of subsidy these figures  reflect dollars give an outright they  don't reflect any sums of matching money  raised against our grants it's a  mechanism we frequently use to  incentivize a leveraging effect for our  funds if we were to calculate that these  numbers would be much much higher the  point here is to emphasize that the  foundation has initiated and sustained  funding over time we do not simply  respond to ad hoc requests or  specialized interests we've operated  with an intentional and incremental  incremental capacity  building strategy that has allowed these  universities to overtime better sustain  the necessary training infrastructure  working in close connection with Museum  Library and heritage communities and as  Debbie so eloquently said these are  collaborative competitors the programs  work very closely together and I think  we intend through our funding to help  reinforce that idea that the programs  together serve as a cohort even though  they do during admissions season get  very competitive despite the  long-standing focus and dollars spent it  would be misleading to say that  conservation training and practice  exists in isolation the foundation has  maintained a deep interest in fostering  interdisciplinary practices in the  humanities including promoting the use  of objects as the basis for scholarly  research the more fluent our future art  historians and curators are in working  directly with objects the more  productive and equitable the dialogue  with conservation colleagues becomes and  the more new knowledge is produced this  slide shows a selection of universities  where this kind of interdisciplinary  curriculum is being enacted increasingly  drawing on conservators and there are  scientists counterparts from campuses or  in partnering museums as teachers  because they hold so much specialized  knowledge concerning materials  techniques identification of process and  methods of examination and analysis the  campuses that house conservation  programs are highlighted here in red to  show it's no coincidence there might be  an increased capacity and appetite for  object study in these environments it's  the Foundation's continued expectation  that by drawing future curators and  scholars into closer connection with  conservation counterparts in earlier  stages of their professional formation  they will begin to craft more  sophisticated questions feel more  comfortable seeking out conservation and  scientific expertise and produced more  robust and collaborative forms of  scholarship further i'll just say on a  personal note it's my personal wish that  conservators continue to achieve more  equitable status with their historically  privileged carrot oriole counterparts  within cultural institutions precisely  because of the  of their specialized knowledge as I  conclude this is the promised snapshot  of our internationally based programs  the foundation has recently supported  most of which you've already heard  mentioned in earlier talks from my  distinguished colleagues who can all  speak to them in much greater depth than  I could so please raise questions during  the discussion period if you're  interested the foundation takes a  limited stance in international  grant-making but we occasionally seize  on opportunities where collaboration and  some targeted grant programs can have an  outsized impact on particular identified  needs I thank you all for your attention  and look forward to the discussion
 

